      "title": "Educational Content in Iran: Digital Learning and Accessibility",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s educational media, virtual learning policies, and accessible textbook initiatives, with distinctions between plans and verified delivery."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "learning-across-media",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning materials across different media",
        "content": "Educational content is the material through which a lesson is communicated: an explanation, illustration, exercise, recording, or interactive activity. In Iran, the documented institutional landscape extends beyond electronic copies of books. UNESCO’s education technology profile identifies television, radio, and the Shad online channel among the tools used for distance education during the COVID-19 response. These are different delivery routes, rather than interchangeable measures of educational quality. A broadcast can distribute a lesson; a virtual classroom can also support exchanges between teachers and pupils. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader, the important distinction is between a learning resource and the system carrying it. Shad is a student education network, while a textbook, recorded explanation, or exercise is content. Describing a platform’s existence does not establish that every learner can obtain suitable material or use it successfully. The inspected policy profile documents intended functions, rather than an independent evaluation of their results. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "curriculum-and-teachers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Curriculum alignment and teacher participation",
        "content": "UNESCO’s account of Iranian policy describes priorities for electronic textbook access and standards for producing educational resources. It also summarizes a 2022 Shad requirements document that calls for content production and editing, interactive virtual classes, and stronger digital literacy among teachers and students. These provisions connect content creation with classroom practice: distributing files is only part of the educational task. They are documented policy expectations, not proof of uniform implementation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accessible-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Accessibility changes the content itself",
        "content": "UNICEF Iran identifies inclusive digital learning platforms and digital textbooks among its education interventions, with attention to children with disabilities and refugee children. Its programme description places these resources alongside classroom expansion and support for pupils’ learning and retention. Digital publishing is therefore presented as one component of educational inclusion, rather than a substitute for schools and teachers. [s2]\n\nA UNICEF consultancy document hosted by the United Nations in Iran gives a more concrete example. It describes HAMGAM, an accessible and inclusive digital learning platform being developed with the Special Education Organization. The document reports an interface and initial demonstration, while commissioning further development, testing, content adaptation, and deployment. It also describes earlier support for multimedia production facilities and teacher training. This establishes institutional activity, but does not establish completion of the commissioned platform. [s3]\n\nThe same document explains why accessibility requires editorial decisions as well as software. Narration, sign language, image descriptions, and interactive alternatives can convey information that a conventional page leaves inaccessible. Its proposed development process includes consultation with users and pilot testing. The practical implication is that a lesson must be reviewed in the forms learners will actually encounter, rather than judged only by its visual appearance. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence can establish",
        "content": "These sources support an account of educational content as a combination of curriculum, production skills, distribution, and accessibility. They do not establish nationwide learning gains attributable to a particular platform. UNICEF’s programme statement expresses objectives, while the HAMGAM terms of reference distinguish an initial demonstration from work still being commissioned. Readers should preserve those differences when assessing claims about digital education in Iran. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
