    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Digital advertising in Iran includes paid placements on websites and inside applications, with domestic providers offering several formats and targeting services. Understanding the field requires separating an advertised service from verified campaign performance and distinguishing domestic internet restrictions from international platform policies.",
    "tags": ["Digital advertising", "Advertising networks", "Web advertising", "Mobile applications", "Platform policy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:44:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:44:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Digital Advertising in Iran: Formats, Networks and Access",
      "description": "Learn how Iranian digital advertising services describe web and app placements, payment models, targeting, and international platform restrictions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "formats-and-intermediaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Paid placements on websites and in applications",
        "content": "Digital advertising places a commercial message within an online service. The advertiser supplies the offer, while a publisher provides space or access to an audience; an advertising platform may connect them. Iranian providers’ catalogues show both website and application placements. Yektanet also describes publisher monetization, illustrating the connection between advertisers seeking exposure and media owners seeking revenue. [s1]\n\nFormats vary. Tapsell lists banner, video, native, push-notification, and application advertising services. Native advertising is presented in a form that resembles the surrounding publication, while a banner is a separate visual placement. These are format distinctions, not evidence that one produces better results in every setting. The provider’s catalogue is evidence of its marketed services, rather than an independent comparison. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "payment-and-targeting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Payment models and targeting describe different things",
        "content": "A format describes how an advertisement appears; a payment model describes what triggers a charge. Tapsell describes click advertising as a service in which the advertiser pays for clicks. Yektanet’s application advertising description names installation, click, and impression payment bases. Those events are different: displaying an advertisement, opening its destination, and installing an application are not interchangeable outcomes. [s1][s2]\n\nTargeting is another layer. Yektanet describes selecting audiences using behaviour, location, and interests, alongside retargeting services. Such descriptions establish the company’s offer, but do not independently verify targeting accuracy or incremental sales. A report of campaign activity therefore needs a clear account of what was counted before it can support a claim of business success. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international-policy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International services have their own eligibility rules",
        "content": "Google’s inspected policy states that Google Ads is unavailable to advertisers in Iran. It also says that physical presence in an embargoed region prevents account sign-in and that sanctioned countries cannot be selected for location targeting. These are Google’s stated product restrictions; they should not be generalized into a claim that every foreign digital service follows identical rules. [s3]\n\nThe policy also distinguishes specific location targeting from globally targeted campaigns. Consequently, seeing an advertisement while in Iran does not by itself establish that Iran was selected as its target. Likewise, an intermediary’s marketing offer cannot establish eligibility for every account or campaign. Provider descriptions and platform policy answer different questions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access and accountability remain separate tests",
        "content": "Domestic access conditions matter alongside international policies. Freedom House documented filtering and connectivity disruptions in its 2024 Iran report. Those findings explain a historical distribution risk, but do not establish which services were reachable everywhere at the later editorial cutoff. [s4]\n\nCommercial accountability is a further issue. The Electronic Commerce Act text hosted by WIPO Lex addresses misleading advertising, clear product information, advertiser identity, and consumer choice over postal or email advertising. It also contains provisions concerning certain sensitive personal data. These are provisions in the inspected text, not a complete current compliance assessment. [s5]\n\nTogether, these distinctions make digital advertising easier to interpret: format, charging event, audience selection, access, and commercial responsibility each describe a different part of the activity. Evidence that a service exists should be kept separate from evidence that a campaign reached its intended audience or improved sales. [s1][s2][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
